hen  they have have complications and  symptoms amber rose isaac shamani gibson  two women whose families we follow in  the film they went looking for help  and they were dismissed when they had  when they were expressing their symptoms  well they went to the hospital neil and  they were complaining about the symptoms  short shortness of breath i can't  breathe chest pains and time after time  in one case  the medical staff on three different  occasions says is she on drugs does she  do drugs why are black women not believe  do you think  tanya said that the health care system  is built on racism and one of the things  that i like to remind myself and remind  others of often is that  a bad system will beat a good person  every time  and you know bias is sort of built into  the way  doctors are trained uh were doctors are  trained to be biased what do you mean  they're not well you know there's a  really thin line between clinical  intuition which is celebrated which is  like looking at someone and knowing if  they're sick or not and what's  effectively racism  so you think it has to do with training  i think it 100 has to do with training  yeah now abortion isn't mentioned in  this film but the overturning of roe v  wade is the backdrop in which people are  watching this so when you think about  the connection between abortion access  safe births and also the overall  maternal mortality crisis  how do you see that all almost  laying together yeah i'm deeply  concerned i'm deeply concerned for black  women i'm deeply concerned about delay  of care when black women have  complications that they may have delayed  care i'm deeply concerned about the  criminalization of women  when there are miscarriages  i'm deeply concerned that that black  women will be criminalized i just think  that we are in a system in a situation  where the autonomy of women's bodies is  in in conversation and we are already in  a crisis around maternal health and the  dobbs decision just is going to  exacerbate it i believe there was a  study that just came out that 21 that  that maternal death is going to go up 21  given this decision 33 for black women  i don't think people realize this is a  crisis though guys there's a line in the  duck that really made the hair stand up  on my neck where it says black women  having a baby is like a black man in a  traffic stop what do you mean by that  well i think you got to pay attention  every step of the way  again when we as black people are in  certain systems whether it's the  criminal justice system or the health  care system as neil just talked about  there is a bias against brown and black  people and so you need to be paying  attention to who is dealing with you and  how they're perceiving you and making  sure that you advocate for yourself in  the best way possible in other words one  wrong move and it could cost you your  life absolutely you know dr shah one of  the things that i found fascinating in  the documentary is you talk about  cesarean sections and how before 1970  less than five percent of women who were  delivering had cesarean section  c-sections and it's risen by 30 in 2019  but the outcomes have remained the same  we're talking major surgery and yet  hospitals make more money if they  perform c-sections and spend less time  in the operating room with a patient  break that down for our audience that's  exactly right so since the 1970s  c-section rates have gone up by 500  percent it's designed to be a  life-saving surgery but the reason that  we're here is that maternal mortality is  going in the wrong direction it's going  up  uh and term infants are zero percent  better off uh on average over that time  period  and uh there's a lot of conventional  wisdom  about what's driving c-section rates but  they're only it's higher for black women  yeah it is even higher for black star  which i think is really revealing right  it's not necessarily that people are  demanding more c-sections it's that  people are investing less than  supporting people and labor which is why  the film depicts an alternate way to  support people but but yet you all say  that you want this film to be empowering  because i tell you i i choked up a  couple of times when you see the women  before they had their babies so full of  life and joy yes and what their husbands  are dealing with very excited to be  mothers excited yeah they were so ready  vlad  so i choked up but then it made me so  angry but you say tanya you want the  takeaway to be empowering i do i do  first of all i i think it is must-see  yes well first of all i think it's  really important that you feel for these  families and we needed to put a human  face  on on the women who are dying we read  the statistics but they're not numbers  they're people so you have to understand  who they are um but then on the other  hand you know we do show an amazing  birth in the film  by a woman who really decided i mean she  thought she was going to do the regular  thing and and then sort of made a  decision to see what other options are  out there for her so i would say i want  the film to be empowering because i want  women to come away and families to  understand there's choice in birthing  you can birth in many different ways you  don't necessarily have to do it at a  hospital  and that there are things that we all  can do to improve birthing outcomes and  it's really incumbent upon us to figure  out what that is well you gave us a lot  to think about one woman in the film  shimani makiba gibson who's no longer  with us we will not forget you i mean  it's very powerful and very well done  bravo to you both thank you thank you  the name of the documentary is called  aftershock it's streaming now on hulu so  you can actually see it today

